<section class="section" id="method">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="tag">How we tested</span>
    <h2 class="sec-title">One workflow, every tool</h2>
    <p class="sec-lead">We ran the same five real writing tasks on every tool, blog intro, ad hook, rewrite-to-human pass, weekly-report draft, and long-form outline, then graded one thing: does the output sound like a person wrote it?</p>
    <div class="pains">
      <div class="pain">
        <div class="pain-ico">①</div>
        <h3>Same prompts, same tasks</h3>
        <p>Every tool gets the identical brief, so scores are comparable, not vibes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pain">
        <div class="pain-ico">②</div>
        <h3>Graded on "human?"</h3>
        <p>Not feature count. Not marketing claims. Does a real reader believe a person wrote it?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pain">
        <div class="pain-ico">③</div>
        <h3>Fact-checked against reality</h3>
        <p>We verify every claim against the live tool and real screenshots before it reaches you.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="audiences">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="tag">Who it's for</span>
    <h2 class="sec-title">Pick by the work you actually do</h2>
    <p class="sec-lead">The right tool depends on what you write every day. Start from your job, not from a feature list.</p>
    <div class="tool-grid">
      <a class="tcard" href="for-office-workers.html"><span class="tlogo">Office workers</span><p>Weekly reports, emails, slides. Clear and factual, no invented wins.</p></a>
      <a class="tcard" href="for-content-creators.html"><span class="tlogo">Content creators</span><p>Blog posts, newsletters, scripts. Scale output without losing your voice.</p></a>
      <a class="tcard" href="for-self-media.html"><span class="tlogo">Self-media</span><p>Hooks and captions for Xiaohongshu, Douyin, TikTok, WeChat.</p></a>
      <a class="tcard" href="for-beginners.html"><span class="tlogo">Beginners</span><p>Never used an AI writer. Start here, no jargon, no overwhelm.</p></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="tag">The second trap</span>
    <h2 class="sec-title">The factual traps hidden inside AI writing tools</h2>
    <p class="sec-lead">But AI tools bring their own traps. The most dangerous ones are factual: they make up sources, statistics, or product specs that never existed; they write about features, prices, and policies that are already outdated because their training data has a cutoff; and they pin one tool's features onto another. In our reviews we fight all three, every time.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="blog/how-we-avoid-ai-hallucinations.html">Read how we handle factual errors →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="tag">About</span>
    <h2 class="sec-title">Why this site exists</h2>
    <div class="about-card">
      <p>I'm a content creator, and writing the weekly report at my day job used to be my biggest headache. Every day is spent wrestling with words. Before AI, an article could take hours or even days. AI shrank that dramatically, but leaning on it alone brings its own problems.</p>
      <p>So I'm publishing my real, hands-on experience with every AI tool I use on this site: the details, the pitfalls, the cost, what alternatives exist, and how each stacks up against the others.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

